Task 2 — Healthcare Patient Data Preprocessing
Overview
This notebook (`devixo_task_2_huz.ipynb`) takes a raw healthcare patient dataset and runs it through a full data cleaning and preprocessing pipeline: missing-value imputation, duplicate removal, outlier handling, type conversion, categorical encoding, feature scaling, and feature engineering — finishing with an equivalent automated `scikit-learn` pipeline as a bonus.
Dataset
File: `healthcare_patient_dataset_clean_2000_records.csv`
Records: 2,000 patients
Columns: 15
`Patient_ID`, `Age`, `Gender`, `Blood_Type`, `City`, `Department`
`Admission_Date`, `Diagnosis`, `Treatment`, `Medication`
`Stay_Days`, `Medical_Cost`, `Insurance`, `Satisfaction_Score`, `Order_Status`
Environment / Requirements
Google Colab (uses `google.colab.drive` to mount the dataset from Google Drive)
Python 3
`pandas`, `numpy`, `matplotlib`, `seaborn`, `scikit-learn`
To run outside Colab, replace the `drive.mount(...)` cell and the `dataset_path` variable with a local file path.
Workflow / Notebook Structure
Load & inspect — mount Drive, load CSV, check `shape` (2000×15) and `columns`.
Missing values
Detected: `Age` (12), `City` (10), `Medication` (294), `Medical_Cost` (10), `Satisfaction_Score` (8).
Visualized with a bar chart before handling.
Imputed: numeric columns (`Age`, `Medical_Cost`, `Satisfaction_Score`) with median; categorical columns (`City`, `Medication`) with mode.
Re-checked and visualized: 0 missing values after handling.
Duplicates — checked and removed (0 found; shape unchanged at 2000×15).
Outlier detection & treatment
IQR method applied to all numeric columns.
`Stay_Days`: 4 outliers capped; `Medical_Cost`: 4 outliers capped; `Age` and `Satisfaction_Score`: no outliers.
Before/after box plots for `Stay_Days` and `Medical_Cost`.
Data type conversion — `Admission_Date` converted from `object` to `datetime64[ns]`.
Categorical encoding — One-hot encoding (`pd.get_dummies`, `drop_first=True`) applied to 9 categorical columns (`Gender`, `Blood_Type`, `City`, `Department`, `Diagnosis`, `Treatment`, `Medication`, `Insurance`, `Order_Status`), expanding the dataset from 15 to 46 columns.
Standardization — `StandardScaler` applied to `Age`, `Stay_Days`, `Medical_Cost`, `Satisfaction_Score` (mean ≈ 0, std ≈ 1 after scaling). Distributions visualized before/after.
Feature engineering — two new features derived from `Admission_Date`:
`Day_of_Week`
`Month`
(Three additional planned features — sales revenue, profit per unit, sales-per-rating — were not applicable to this dataset, since it has no unit price/quantity/rating columns; this is noted explicitly in the notebook.) Final shape: 2000 × 48.
Descriptive statistics before vs. after preprocessing — full `.describe()` comparison table.
Written explanation of all 7 preprocessing steps (missing values, duplicates, outliers, data types, encoding, standardization, feature engineering) with rationale for each.
Bonus: automated `scikit-learn` pipeline — a `ColumnTransformer` combining:
Numeric pipeline: `SimpleImputer(strategy='median')` → `StandardScaler`
Categorical pipeline: `SimpleImputer(strategy='most_frequent')` → `OneHotEncoder(drop='first', handle_unknown='ignore')`
Applied to 6 numeric + 9 categorical features → output shape (2000, 46), plus a small bar chart summarizing the 4 pipeline stages.
Key Results
Step	Before	After
Missing values	334 total across 5 columns	0
Duplicate rows	0	0
Outliers (Stay_Days, Medical_Cost)	4 + 4	capped to IQR bounds
Columns	15	48 (after encoding + feature engineering)
Age / Stay_Days / Medical_Cost / Satisfaction_Score scale	raw units	standardized (mean 0, std 1)
How to Reproduce
Open the notebook in Google Colab (or Jupyter).
Update `dataset_path` to point to your copy of `healthcare_patient_dataset_clean_2000_records.csv`.
Run all cells top to bottom (manual pipeline first, automated `sklearn` pipeline in the bonus section at the end).
